{"tool": "finish", "summary": "Added a stored word count for entries and exposed it in the API as `word_count`. Build, 53 unit-test packages and 102 integration tests pass; the new behaviour itself is not covered by existing tests.\n\n- **Schema**: new migration adds a nullable `entries.word_count int` column. Existing entries are left null (no backfill).\n- **Saving**: `createEntry`, `updateEntry` and `UpdateEntryTitleAndContent` in `internal/storage/entry.go` compute the count, write it, and set it on the in-memory entry. The last matters because the API update handler returns that same entry object; I updated that function's `@effects` tag to include `mut`.\n- **Counting rule**: tags are stripped with the existing `sanitizer.StripTags`, then whitespace-separated words are counted \u2014 the same approach the reading-time estimate uses. One consequence: adjacent block elements with no whitespace between them, e.g. `<p>hello</p><p>world</p>`, count as one word.\n- **Reading**: `fetchEntries` selects `e.word_count` into the new `WordCount *int` field on `model.Entry`, so it serialises as an integer or null.\n- **Client**: `client.Entry` gained the same `WordCount *int` field.\n\nThe new helper `countContentWords` has no `@effects` tag, since `StripTags` itself is untagged.", "t": 1794461789.267}
